<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>2.13.6: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00615</md:content-id>
    <md:title>2.13.6: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>740dfc49-7550-4f67-b87b-8a630f6addda</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="d9e6701c-63d6-41fa-bc53-e04b99537fae"><problem><para>
      <blockquote>
        <para>This year, students in the 9th grade are collecting dimes and quarters for a school fundraiser. They are trying to collect more money than the students who were in the 9th grade last year. The students in 9th grade last year collected $143.88.</para>
      </blockquote>
      <para>Using \(d\) to represent the number of dimes collected and \(q\) to represent the number of quarters, which statement best represents this situation?</para>
      </para><para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(0.25d + 0.1q ≥ 143.88\)</para><para>\(0.25q + 0.1d &lt; 143.88\)</para><para>\(0.25d + 0.1q &gt; 143.88\)</para><para>\(0.25q + 0.1d &gt; 143.88\)</para></problem><solution><para>\(0.25q + 0.1d &gt; 143.88\)</para><para>
      <para>The correct answer is \(0.25q + 0.1d &gt;143.88\). </para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section><section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="87928af0-7213-40e9-8c7f-ec5beaaeb638"><problem><para>
      <para>Can Amira purchase 2 pounds of dried chili peppers and 4 pounds of corn husks
          and spend less than $50? Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>
      </para></problem><solution><para>No</para><para>
      <para>The correct answer is No. She would be spending \(16.95(2) + 6.49(4)\), or $59.86. </para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section><section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="8fa1b4ce-573c-418c-8d99-7cf6deb543a9"><problem><para>
      <para>Can Amira purchase 1.5 pounds of dried chili peppers and 3 pounds of corn
          husks and spend less than $50? Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>
      </para></problem><solution><para>Yes</para><para>
      <para>The correct answer is Yes. She would be spending \(16.95(1.5) + 6.49(3)\), or $44.90. </para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section><section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="a9937132-55c8-4ecf-ac34-62aa3212d11e"><problem><para>
      <para>For 8 - 10, use the passage below to answer the questions:</para>
      <blockquote>
        <para>A beach store rents surfboards for $15 per hour and beach umbrellas for $10 per hour. The store wants to make at least $200 per day on renting beach equipment. Let \(s\) be the number of hours that a surfboard is rented and \(u\) be the number of hours an umbrella is rented.</para>
      </blockquote>
      <para>Which inequality represents this situation?</para>
      </para><para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(15s – 10u ≥ 200\)</para><para>\(15s + 10u ≤ 200\)</para><para>\(15s + 10u ≥ 200\)</para><para>\(15s + 10u &lt; 200\)</para></problem><solution><para>\(15s + 10u ≥ 200\)</para><para>
      <para>The correct answer is \(15s + 10u ≥ 200\). </para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section><section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="be434d8c-84fb-4e60-aeb5-f281d2c0b5fc"><problem><para>
      <para>Can the
          beach store rent surfboards for 7 total hours and rent umbrellas for 9
          total hours and reach their goal of making $200? Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>
      </para></problem><solution><para>No</para><para>
      <para>The correct answer is No. They would be making \(15(7) + 10(9)\), or $195. </para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section><section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="112ec7bb-6ccc-4e5d-a667-9e7644f57e8d"><problem><para>
      <para>Mariah has data in a table. Use desmos to plot the data and find a possible inequality in two variables that could be represented by this data. Which graph represents this inequality?</para>
      <table class="os-raise-skinnytable">
<thead>
         <row>
           <entry><emphasis>\(x\)</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>\(y\)</emphasis></entry></row>
  </thead>
       <tbody>
         <row>
           <entry>−4</entry>
           <entry>7</entry>
         </row>
         <row>
           <entry>−2</entry>
           <entry>3</entry>
         </row>
         <row>
           <entry>0</entry>
           <entry>-1</entry>
         </row>
         <row>
           <entry>2</entry>
           <entry>-5</entry>
         </row>
       </tbody>
     </table>
        
    </para><para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(y &lt; -2x-4\)</para><para>\(y \leq -2x-4\)</para><para>\(y &gt; -2x-4\)</para><para>\(y \leq -2x-4\)</para></problem><solution><para>\(Y &gt; -2x-4\)</para><para>
      <para>The correct answer is \(y &gt; -2x-4\)</para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section></section>
</content>
